From: rcooley <rcooley () spamcop ! net>
Date: Wed, 20 Aug 2003 15:12:44 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: Kernel can't find root device
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=106139358423227

I have a Digital Personal Workstation 500au with IDE drives that has 
been running OpenBSD 3.2 since it came out.  Odly enough, after changing 
the harddrive wiring (system disk was sec master, now primary slave) I 
started getting the error message below:


fd0 at fdc0 drive 0: 1.44MB 80 cyl, 2 head, 18 sec
mcclock0 at isa0 port 0x70/2: mc146818 or compatible
stray isa irq 3
WARNING: can't figure what device matches ""
root device:


This comes from the OpenBSD kernel.  I have updated the boot device in 
the SRM console, and looked through the other options to see if there 
was anything unusual that might be causing the problem, but nothing 
stands out.

If I type: "wd0a", the system does boot up properly, but typing that 
every time is not possible.  In fact, I tried installing OpenBSD on a 
second harddrive, but get the same message, so the problem can't be a 
corrupt kernell/boot sect.

I'm wondering if anyone can offer a pointer here.  Is there some option 
I have not set, or does OpenBSD get confused if the root drive is the 
secondary slave?  Moving the drive back to secondary master will really 
mess-up my system arrangement.

Thanks for any help.

