From openbsd-alpha  Wed Aug 20 17:32:54 2003
From: naddy () mips ! inka ! de (Christian Weisgerber)
Date: Wed, 20 Aug 2003 17:32:54 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: The State of (my) Alpha
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=106140444705492

I'm not sure whether there is an OpenBSD developer left with a
reliably running alpha.  I think I need to expose my particular
pains to a wider audience.

My machine is a PC164SX, always running a rather recent -current.

In recent weeks it suffered frequent crashes but that was during
the European heat wave and since temperatures are back to normal
the box has been running fine again.  So that probably was a heat
issue.

I'm now back to my old problem.  While the machine is generally
fine, _some_ programs cause strange system behavior.  In those
cases, systat vmstat reports an insane number of traps (e.g. ~90000)
and the soft interrupt and fault numbers run into each other.  This
happens reproducibly at least for

  c++                   (i.e. the system C++ compiler)
  mozilla-firebird

Concerning this problem, there are better and worse kernels.  With
the better kernels, the system load gobbles up about two thirds of
the machine's CPU.  With the bad kernels, it's 90%+.  The user space
programs, such as c++, get correspondingly less CPU.  For better
kernels that is annoying, for worse kernels this is debilitating.
Lately, I've only had worse kernels.

People tend to complain about crashes but I have yet to find somebody
who shares this type of problem.

-- 
Christian "naddy" Weisgerber                          naddy@mips.inka.de

