I've been trying to get R running on OpenBSD alpha.
There have been some recent minor problems with getting later versions to
run on OpenBSD/i386, but they've just been autoconfigure related problems
and easily fixed. I've sent patches back to the maintainers of R and
hopefully the next version will compile on OpenBSD without problems.

Despite success with OpenBSD/i386 I've been unable to get R to run properly
on the alpha. It crashes with SIGFPE's on attempting any graphical
operations. R also crashes if an attempt is made to perform the sqrt of a
negative number - where the behaviour on i386 is to produce a "NaN" result
and a warning.

I'm not sure that it's necessarily an R problem. Unfortunately I've been
unsuccessful in trying to pin down the problem although I must admit I'm
not a programmer and probably don't have the experience required to sort
this out properly.

During my investigation I noticed that fpsetmask(0) didn't appear to work
the way I thought it should.

For example,
Given the following:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <ieeefp.h>

int main() {
        int i;

#ifdef IEEE_FPU
        fpsetmask(0);
#endif

        for(i=2; i >= -2; i--) {
                printf("The log of %d is %f\n", i, log(i));
                printf("The square root of %d is %f\n", i, sqrt(i));
        }
#ifdef IEEE_FPU
        fpsetmask(~0);
#endif
        return 0;

Compiled with or without -mieee, and with or without -DIEEE_FPU, the same
result is produced. 
ie:
shiraz:/home/prye/C$ ./fpe
The log of 2 is 0.693147
The square root of 2 is 1.414214
The log of 1 is 0.000000
The square root of 1 is 1.000000
The log of 0 is -Inf
The square root of 0 is 0.000000
Floating point exception (core dumped) 


Is this the expected behaviour? Is it possible to get fully IEEE compliant
floating point operations on OpenBSD/alpha currently?

Am I misunderstanding the use of fpsetmask(), or using it incorrectly?

Any other suggestions re: R on the alpha?

